## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 2D DP with adjacency constraints and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- dp[i][t] for max sum with i elements and t picks
- Transitions handling adjacent restriction
- Base cases and backtracking for indices
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  dp[0][1] = A[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const skip = dp[i-1][t];
      const take = (i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction can store choices in a separate table
  return {sum: dp[n-1][k], indices: []};
}
```

